Setting. The page continues Grothendieck's analysis of 'local' outer automorphisms u of the profinite fundamental groupoid/group of P^1 minus 3 points: Aut_lac(Ĝ^+_{0,3}, σ_∞)', with Autext ≅ Autext(π̂_{0,3})'. These are automorphisms respecting the loop (lacet) classes and the symmetries ρ (order 3) and σ_∞ (order 2). Such a u has a 'multiplier' p = χ(u) ∈ Ẑ^× (the cyclotomic character when u comes from Γ_Q). It also has an exponent γ ∈ Ẑ, and 'characteristic' elements g, g_0, h_0, ξ, λ_0, λ of π̂_{0,3} (ξ is taken modulo L_0). On the previous page he regards these as functions on the group (and so on Γ_Q).

The values on this page fit γ = (p−1)/2. That is exactly Drinfeld's m = (λ−1)/2 in GT^, so the dictionary is p ↔ λ and h_0 / ξ ↔ f.

(75bis): six equivalent conditions all expressing χ(u) = 1:
- p = 1, γ = 0;
- g = σ_∞(g_0)ρ(g_0)^{-1};
- h_0 = ξ;
- σ_∞(ξ) = ξ^{-1};
- h_0ρ(h_0)ρ²(h_0) = 1.

In modern terms, (v) is the 2-cycle relation f(x,y)f(y,x) = 1. (vi) is the 3-cycle (hexagon) relation f(z,x)z^m f(y,z)y^m f(x,y)x^m = 1 in the special case m = 0. He calls (vi) the 'simplified equation in ξ'.

He concludes that finding the multiplier-1 subgroup Aut_lac(...)'^0 amounts to finding simultaneous solutions of his equations (73), equivalently of the parametric 'coboundary' equations (74). This is the analogue of describing the kernel of λ: GT^ → Ẑ^×.

General multiplier. The only explicit nontrivial element he can write is u = τ_∞, complex conjugation. Its data (75ter) are:
- p = −1, γ = −1;
- g = l_1^{-1};
- g_0 = h_0 = 1, ξ = l_0^{-1};
- λ'_0 = 1, λ' = l_0^{-1};
- [λ_0 = τ_∞, λ = l_0^{-1}τ_∞].

This matches the known fact that complex conjugation is (λ, f) = (−1, 1) in GT.

He ends by saying it would be 'no luxury' to write many other explicit u. The aim is to see whether the 'crowds of necessary conditions' he extracted for elements coming from Π_Q (Γ_Q) are automatically satisfied. In modern terms this is the question of whether Γ_Q → GT-type group is surjective (Ihara's question), and the difficulty of exhibiting explicit elements of GT^.
